        <li><a href="https://www.bose.com/p/headphones/bose-quietcomfort-ultra-headphones-2nd-gen/QCUH2-HEADPHONEARN.html" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Bose QuietComfort Ultra Headphones (2nd Gen)</a> — battery 30 h with ANC on (23 h with Immersive Audio), weight 0.583 lb / 264 g, list price $449</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.jabra.com/business/office-headsets/jabra-evolve/jabra-evolve2-85" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Jabra Evolve2 85</a> — battery 32 h with ANC / 37 h without, 10-microphone call array</li>
        <li><a href="https://eu.sennheiser-hearing.com/en-ie/products/momentum-4-wireless" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Sennheiser Momentum 4 Wireless</a> — battery up to 60 h with ANC, 2 microphones per side</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.sony.com/en/electronics/headband-headphones/wh-1000xm4" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Sony WH-1000XM4</a> — battery 30 h with ANC, weight 254 g</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.bose.com/p/headphones/bose-quietcomfort-headphones-2nd-gen/QC2G-HEADPHONEARN.html" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Bose QuietComfort Headphones (2nd Gen)</a> — battery 24 h with ANC on (18 h with Immersive Audio), Bluetooth 5.4 multipoint, 6 microphones, list price $359; Bose publishes no weight for this model. Launch details: <a href="https://www.bose.com/pressroom/bose-updates-the-iconic-quietcomfort-headphones" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Bose press release, 6 August 2026</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.apple.com/airpods-max/specs/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Apple AirPods Max 2</a> — battery 20 h with ANC, weight 386.2 g, nine microphones</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.soundcore.com/products/a3028011" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Soundcore Life Q30</a> — battery 40 h with ANC / 60 h without, weight 260 g</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="disclaimer">Two caveats. First, both Bose rows above are the second-generation models, and those are the figures quoted. The first generation is still in circulation: Bose keeps a separate QuietComfort Headphones page live, that model is rated 24 hours and has no Immersive Audio at all, and the first-generation Ultra is rated 24 hours. Bose currently lists both QuietComfort Headphones generations at $359, so price does not separate them — the model code does, and the 2nd Gen is QC2G. Second, where a vendor publishes a range rather than a single figure (Jabra quotes ANC-on and ANC-off separately), the table shows the ANC-on number because that is the one that matters for a workday.</p>

      <h2 id="which-one">Which One Should You Buy</h2>
      <p><strong>You are on calls most of the day.</strong> Jabra Evolve2 85. A boom microphone is still the most reliable way to sound clear, and the Teams and Zoom certifications matter if your IT department manages your laptop. Accept that you will not want to wear it outside.</p>

      <p><strong>You need one pair for everything.</strong> Sony WH-1000XM5. It is the lightest of the premium options, it holds two devices at once, and its microphone array is the best of the consumer models in this list. The Bose QC Ultra (2nd Gen) is the alternative if music quality outranks calls for you.</p>

      <p><strong>You want the best sound and will pay for it.</strong> Bose QuietComfort Ultra (2nd Gen). Better tuning, Immersive Audio and a 30-hour rating that now matches the Sony — but spatial audio cuts that to 23 hours, and at $449 it is the priciest consumer pair here.</p>

      <p><strong>You want the Bose sound for less.</strong> Bose QuietComfort Headphones (2nd Gen). Immersive Audio, Bluetooth 5.4 multipoint and physical buttons on both earcups for $359, against $449 for the Ultra — you give up CustomTune, the 30-hour rating and the Ultra's stronger ANC, and Immersive Audio itself cuts run time to 18 hours.</p>

      <p><strong>You rarely charge anything.</strong> Sennheiser Momentum 4 Wireless. Sixty hours rated with ANC on is the longest in this list by a wide margin, and the sound is close to the Bose.</p>

      <p><strong>Everything you own is Apple.</strong> AirPods Max 2. Automatic switching and Transparency mode are better than any third-party multipoint, but 386 g is a real daily cost and there is no simultaneous two-device audio.</p>

      <p><strong>Your budget is $100.</strong> Soundcore Life Q30 — provided you work in a quiet room and do not depend on its microphones. If you take calls all day, buy a $40 USB headset for meetings instead and keep the Q30 for music.</p>

      <h2 id="anc-limits">What ANC Can and Cannot Do</h2>
      <p>ANC works by sampling noise with microphones and playing an inverted signal into your ear. That works best on steady, low-frequency sound: engines, fans, air conditioning, road noise. It works worst on speech, which changes too fast for the correction loop and arrives from unpredictable directions.</p>

      <p>This is why an open-plan office is still hard even with a $400 headphone. You will hear the conversation three desks away; it will just be quieter and flatter. Adding music or white noise closes most of that gap, which is why the combination — ANC plus low-level audio — is what most people actually use.</p>

      <p>The seal matters as much as the electronics. Thick glasses temples, long hair and loose earcups break it, and ANC performance drops noticeably before you notice the fit has changed. If you wear glasses, budget time to try a model before committing, or buy from a retailer with a returns window.</p>

